{"allowed_modes":["walk","fly","instantly"],"category":"Orientation","citations":[{"archived_url":"","citation":"Glessner, John J. The Story of a House. Unpublished typescript booklet written for his children George and Frances, signed 'Faithfully your father, John J. Glessner', March 1923. Transcribed in full in HABS No. ILL-1015, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, data pages 7-21 (1963).","source_id":"glessner_story_of_a_house_1923","tier":2,"tier_label":"near-primary recollection","url":"","what_it_does_not_supply":["any dimension","the extent of the ivy or creeper in any year","when the stable became a garage"],"what_it_supplies":["the roof covering as built: red baked tiles, unglazed","the street walls of Wellesley granite; all walls lined with hollow brick","the courtyard elevation of common brick of slightly pinkish colour with grey limestone trimmings, matched in the adjoining house's wall","the courtyard entrance from the alley, used on moving day in December 1887","Virginia creeper on the courtyard walls (carried from the old house, 1887) and Boston ivy on the street front (from plants sent after June 1887)","the service entrance 'through the great arch on Eighteenth Street'; the Eighteenth Street door and back stairs","outside iron-work Bower-Barffed rather than painted"]},{"archived_url":"","citation":"Historic American Buildings Survey. John J. Glessner House (Now Chicago School of Architecture Foundation), 1800 Prairie Avenue. HABS No. ILL-1015. Photographs; Written Historical and Descriptive Data, data pages 1-23 (prepared by David T. Van Zanten, student assistant architect, and Osmund Overby, supervisory architect, August 1963) and addendum data pages 24-25 (inventory of photogrammetric images, 1985-1986). National Park Service; Library of Congress, Prints and Photographs Division.","source_id":"habs_glessner_house_il_1015_data_pages","tier":4,"tier_label":"later scholarship","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/il0118/","what_it_does_not_supply":["